rough design for export process: 

Full agreed export flow

1. Start (each server, one raw file):

Read the lists and the CMIC / E014071 filters, and set 5 % progress.
If there is no active output list, a CMIC or E014071 exclusion query fails, or the database is down:
stop both servers (the other one stops before its next list);
send one mail;
both jobs go KO.

2. Filter and write, per list, in any order:

One main query per list.
The CMIC / E014071 exclusions are queried once per raw file and reused.
The "all targets" rules are applied.
Rows are written in JDBC batches, then the list's entry is added to the filtered poller table.

3. One list fails while filtering or writing:

Mail with the stage and cause.
Its partial rows are removed.
The same server continues with the next lists.
That list's entry on the other server is dropped, so no half file is built.

4. XML step (both servers, every minute):

Each server claims one ready list at a time; the same list is never built twice.
A failed file: mail, and the server continues with the next ready list. Nothing else stops.

5. Progress:

5 % at the start, then 90 % split equally across the lists (filter, delete, write, XML).
The calculation stops at 95 %.
100 % and the end date are set only by the existing service, when all lists of the job have their file.
The same rule applies to 1 or 2 servers, normal export and regeneration.

6. Failure status:

No end date and below 100 %.
The job is kept alive only while it still has work, then turns KO after 30 minutes (no timing tricks).
It is completed manually.

7. Server crash or restart: KO, then manual regeneration.

8. Messages: stage, list, file type, version, job and the real cause (for example the ORA code), in the log and in the mail.

9. Filtering performance and safety fixes: kept as delivered:

multi-regime fix;
safe TO_NUMBER;
field whitelist;
FK indexes;
JDBC batch writes;
one-query XML read;
O(n) sanction rule


Technical cause (log only)	Reason in the mail
ORA-xxxxx, SQL or connection error	database error
Invalid field or operator in a filter	invalid filter definition
Number or format problem in the data	invalid data in the SIX file
XSD validation failed	XML file not valid against the schema
DJ IN folder, disk or move error	file could not be written to the DJ IN folder
Anything else	unexpected technical error
